===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|NL.|Prūssia}}<!--not 'Borussia'-->, a Latinization used by Peter of Dusburg of a {{der|en|bat}} ({{der|en|prg|-}}, or perhaps {{der|en|lt|-}} or {{der|en|lv|-}}) autonym.  The {{der|en|ine-pro|-}} source of the name is unclear; more at [[w:Old Prussians#Etymology|Prussia]]. Compare the {{cog|ine-bsl-pro|*prus-sk-}},<ref name="d2008">{{R:sla:EDSIL|423}}</ref> whose cognates include {{cog|sla-pro|*prъskati||to splutter, to splash}},  {{cog|sa|प्रुष्णोति|tr=pruṣṇóti||to sprinkle}},<ref name="d2008"></ref>  and thus signifying "watery land".

The {{cog|enm|-}} designation for the region, {{m|enm|Pruce}}, derives from the same Latinization and is the source of the terms {{m|en|pruce}} and {{m|en|spruce}}.

